   "source": [
    "**Q: Describe and justify the layers, feature-map dimensions, and parameter counts.**\n",
    "\n",
    "We implement the CIFAR-10 adaptations from Part A9 with plain PyTorch layers (no pretrained weights and no torchvision model constructors).\n",
    "\n",
    "AlexNet has five 3x3 convolutions with channels 64->192->384->256->256, stride 1 and padding 1, interleaved with three 2x2 max-pools, then a classifier 4096->1024->512->10 with dropout 0.5. After the pools the feature map is 256x4x4, which flattens to 4096 units. This network has about 6.98M parameters. Compared with the original ImageNet AlexNet we keep the smaller 3x3 stride-1 stem suited to 32x32 inputs, drop local response normalization, and use a thinner head for ten classes.\n",
    "\n",
    "ResNet-18 uses a 3x3 stride-1 stem without the original 7x7 convolution or stem max-pool, then four stages of two basic blocks with widths 64->128->256->512 (stride 2 at the start of stages 2-4), global average pooling and a 512->10 linear layer. It has about 11.17M parameters. Convolutions followed by BatchNorm are created with `bias=False`, since BatchNorm already provides a learned shift.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Q: Describe and justify the input preparation and classification head.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Both architectures consume the same Task 1 inputs: images are converted to float tensors in [0, 1] and normalized with the mean and standard deviation estimated on the training split only. We keep resolution at 32x32, so no ImageNet-style upsampling is required.\n",
    "\n",
    "AlexNet's classification head is the fully connected stack 4096->1024->512->10 with dropout 0.5 between the hidden layers. ResNet replaces that wide MLP with global average pooling over the final 512x4x4 map and a single linear layer to 10 logits. In both cases the network returns raw logits and `cross_entropy` applies the log-softmax internally.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Q: Describe and justify the convolution operations, and the roles they play in the visual task.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The 3x3 convolutions combine nearby pixels (and later nearby feature responses) into new channel activations, which is how the networks build cues for CIFAR object categories. With padding 1, a stride-1 convolution keeps height and width unchanged, so capacity goes into channels rather than shrinking the map too early. Downsampling is done explicitly: AlexNet uses 2x2 max-pooling, while ResNet uses stride-2 convolutions at the beginning of later stages. That reduction increases the receptive field, so deeper layers integrate information over larger regions of the 32x32 image.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Q: Describe and justify the optimization and checkpoint-selection procedure.**\n",
    "\n",
    "We train both models under one shared budget so the comparison is controlled: SGD with learning rate 0.01 and momentum 0.9, cosine decay over 20 epochs, batch size 64, seed 2223, and the same 45,000 / 5,000 train/validation split from Task 1. This baseline uses no data augmentation and no weight decay; AlexNet still uses the dropout from the A9 design. Equal epochs give both models the same number of training examples (900,000 visits including repeats), not equal wall-clock time.\n",
    "\n",
    "After every epoch we measure validation accuracy and keep the checkpoint with the highest value, breaking ties with lower validation cross-entropy. The official test set is never used to pick a checkpoint.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Q: Describe and justify the role of residual connections in ResNet.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Each basic block computes a residual branch `F(x)` and adds it to a shortcut of `x`, then applies ReLU. When channel count and spatial size match, the shortcut is the identity; when the block downsamples or widens, the shortcut is a 1x1 convolution (with BatchNorm) that matches shapes. The skip path lets the block learn a residual correction instead of a full mapping and keeps a direct route for gradients through the stack, which is the main reason this deeper ResNet remains trainable compared with a plain conv stack of similar depth. This experiment still compares full architectures (width, BatchNorm, dropout), so it does not isolate skip connections by themselves.\n"
   ]
  },
